<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>floral-diversity</title></head><body><h1>floral-diversity</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Introduction: Three domains of life, binomial nomenclatur…</li><li>Three Domains and Classification</li><li>Fungi â€” General Features and Types</li><li>Fungi</li><li>Status of flora in Nepal and world representation</li><li>Algae</li><li>Fungi: General introduction and characteristic features o…</li><li>Bryophyta — Liverworts, Hornworts and Mosses</li><li>Bryophytes</li><li>Structure and reproduction of Mucor and Yeast</li><li>Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous and non-poisonous mu…</li><li>Pteridophyta — Ferns and Allies</li><li>Pteridophytes</li><li>Gymnosperm â€” Pinus Structure and Reproduction</li><li>Gymnosperms</li><li>Lichen: General introduction, characteristic features and…</li><li>Algae: General introduction and characteristic features o…</li><li>Angiosperm â€” Morphology and Taxonomic Study</li><li>Angiosperms</li><li>Bryophyta: General introduction and characteristic featur…</li><li>Pteridophyta: General introduction and characteristic fea…</li><li>Gymnosperm: General introduction and characteristic featu…</li><li>Angiosperm: Morphology of root, stem, leaves, inflorescen…</li><li>Taxonomic study: Definition, taxonomic hierarchy, classif…</li><li>Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicacea…</li></ul><h2>Introduction: Three domains of life, binomial nomenclatur…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Binomial nomenclature.** Carolus Linnaeus gave the two-word naming system: the first word is the capitalised genus and the second the lowercase species, italicised or underlined — for example, Homo sapiens.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Five-kingdom classification.** R.H. Whittaker grouped life into Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia using cell structure, thallus organisation, nutrition and reproduction as criteria.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Three domains.** Carl Woese's rRNA studies split living organisms into Archaea, Bacteria and Eukarya; Monera falls in the prokaryotic domains while the other kingdoms lie in Eukarya.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Kingdom criteria.** Prokaryotic cell structure places an organism in Monera, eukaryotic unicellular forms in Protista, absorptive heterotrophs in Fungi, and photosynthetic or ingestive eukaryotes in Plantae and Animalia.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Taxonomic hierarchy.** Every organism is placed in a sequence of categories — Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species — with species as the basic unit of classification.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Introduction</td></tr><tr><td>• Three domains of life</td></tr><tr><td>• Binomial nomenclature</td></tr><tr><td>• Five kingdom classification system</td></tr><tr><td>• Monera</td></tr><tr><td>• Protista</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Introduction" as one blob → ✅ split into: introduction, three domains of life, binomial nomenclature.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ iupac: identify parent chain (longest), number from end nearer the functional group / first substituent; suffix by priority (−ol, −al, −one, −oic acid…)..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Introduction: Three domains of life, binomial nomenclatur… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Introduction, Three domains of life, binomial nomenclature. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Three Domains and Classification</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Three-domain system (Woese, 1977): Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya — based on 16S rRNA gene sequences.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Five-kingdom classification (Whittaker, 1969): Monera (prokaryotes), Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia — based on cell type and mode of nutrition.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Binomial nomenclature (Linnaeus, 1753): Genus species (italicised, e.g. Rhododendron arboreum).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Taxonomic hierarchy: Domain > Kingdom > Phylum/Division > Class > Order > Family > Genus > Species.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Plantae: eukaryotic, autotrophic, cell wall (cellulose); includes algae, bryophytes, pteridophytes, gymnosperms, angiosperms.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Plant evolution: algae → bryophytes (non-vascular) → pteridophytes (seedless vascular) → gymnosperms (naked seeds) → angiosperms (flowers + enclosed seeds).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Alternation of generations: haploid gametophyte ↔ diploid sporophyte; in bryophytes the gametophyte is dominant; in seed plants the sporophyte is dominant.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Three domains: Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya (based on 16S rRNA).</td></tr><tr><td>Five kingdoms: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia (Whittaker, 1969).</td></tr><tr><td>Taxonomic hierarchy: 8 ranks from Domain to Species.</td></tr><tr><td>Binomial nomenclature: Genus species (italicised, two-part Latin name).</td></tr><tr><td>Alternation of generations: key feature of all land plants.</td></tr><tr><td>Plant evolution: algae → bryophytes → pteridophytes → gymnosperms → angiosperms.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Five-kingdom (Whittaker) separates fungi from plants; the older two-kingdom (Linnaeus) placed fungi under plants.</td></tr><tr><td>Three-domain and five-kingdom are complementary: domains refer to genetic/cell structure; kingdoms to ecological role.</td></tr><tr><td>In bryophytes, the Gametophyte (n) is the dominant, visible generation; in seed plants the Sporophyte (2n) is dominant.</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes have true vascular tissue (xylem + phloem) but no seeds; bryophytes lack true vascular tissue.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms produce "naked" seeds in cones; angiosperms produce seeds enclosed in fruit.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Modern taxonomy classifies life using two complementary systems: the three-domain system (Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya) based on molecular evidence, and the five-kingdom system (Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia) based on cell structure and nutrition. The taxonomic hierarchy (Domain → Species) and binomial nomenclature (Genus species) provide universal naming. Plant evolution shows a progressive transition from simple algae to complex angiosperms, with alternation of generations as a unifying feature.</p><hr><h2>Fungi â€” General Features and Types</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Fungi: eukaryotic, heterotrophic (absorptive nutrition), cell wall of chitin, no chloroplasts; include moulds, yeasts, mushrooms.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Four major groups: Phycomycetes (lower fungi, e.g. Rhizopus, water moulds), Ascomycetes (sac fungi, e.g. Penicillium, yeast, morels), Basidiomycetes (club fungi, e.g. Agaricus, rusts, smuts), Deuteromycetes (imperfect fungi, no known sexual stage, e.g. Aspergillus, Candida).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Body: mycelium (mass of hyphae); hyphae may be septate (divided) or coenocytic (aseptate).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (spores, budding, fragmentation) and sexual (karyogamy after plasmogamy).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ascomycetes produce spores in sac-like asci; Basidiomycetes produce spores on basidia (club-shaped bodies).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Economic importance: decomposers, food (bread, cheese, mushrooms), medicine (antibiotics — penicillin), industry (enzymes, organic acids); harmful: mycoses, crop blights, aflatoxins.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Fungi are eukaryotic, heterotrophic, chitin cell wall, no chloroplasts.</td></tr><tr><td>Four groups: Phycomycetes, Ascomycetes, Basidiomycetes, Deuteromycetes.</td></tr><tr><td>Body = mycelium (hyphae); reproduction = asexual (spores/budding) + sexual.</td></tr><tr><td>Ascomycetes → asci; Basidiomycetes → basidia.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic roles: decomposers, food, medicine, industry; harmful: diseases, crop blights.</td></tr><tr><td>Fungi ≠ plants: heterotrophic, chitin wall, absorptive nutrition.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Fungi have a chitin cell wall (like arthropods), NOT cellulose (like plants).</td></tr><tr><td>Fungi are NOT plants: they are heterotrophic, lack chlorophyll, and are in a separate kingdom.</td></tr><tr><td>Deuteromycetes are "imperfect" fungi — they have lost the sexual stage; they are not a true phylogenetic group.</td></tr><tr><td>Yeast (Saccharomyces) is an Ascomycete; it reproduces by budding (asexual) but also by forming ascospores.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhizopus (bread mould) is a Phycomycete; it reproduces by sporangiospores, not by sexual spores in the lab.</td></tr><tr><td>Mushrooms (Agaricus) are Basidiomycetes; the visible mushroom is the fruiting body of the mycelium.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Fungi are a distinct kingdom of eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms with chitin cell walls and absorptive nutrition. They are classified into four major groups — Phycomycetes (lower, aseptate), Ascomycetes (sac fungi), Basidiomycetes (club fungi), and Deuteromycetes (imperfect). Their roles as decomposers, food sources, medicine producers (antibiotics), and industrial agents make them ecologically and economically vital. NEB examinations focus on the four-group classification, structure of Penicillium, and economic importance.</p><hr><h2>Fungi</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Fungi: eukaryotic, heterotrophic (absorptive nutrition), cell wall of chitin, no chloroplasts; include moulds, yeasts, mushrooms.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Four major groups: Phycomycetes (lower fungi, e.g. Rhizopus, water moulds), Ascomycetes (sac fungi, e.g. Penicillium, yeast, morels), Basidiomycetes (club fungi, e.g. Agaricus, rusts, smuts), Deuteromycetes (imperfect fungi, no known sexual stage, e.g. Aspergillus, Candida).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Body: mycelium (mass of hyphae); hyphae may be septate (divided) or coenocytic (aseptate).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (spores, budding, fragmentation) and sexual (karyogamy after plasmogamy).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ascomycetes produce spores in sac-like asci; Basidiomycetes produce spores on basidia (club-shaped bodies).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Economic importance: decomposers, food (bread, cheese, mushrooms), medicine (antibiotics — penicillin), industry (enzymes, organic acids); harmful: mycoses, crop blights, aflatoxins.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Fungi are eukaryotic, heterotrophic, chitin cell wall, no chloroplasts.</td></tr><tr><td>Four groups: Phycomycetes, Ascomycetes, Basidiomycetes, Deuteromycetes.</td></tr><tr><td>Body = mycelium (hyphae); reproduction = asexual (spores/budding) + sexual.</td></tr><tr><td>Ascomycetes → asci; Basidiomycetes → basidia.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic roles: decomposers, food, medicine, industry; harmful: diseases, crop blights.</td></tr><tr><td>Fungi ≠ plants: heterotrophic, chitin wall, absorptive nutrition.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Fungi have a chitin cell wall (like arthropods), NOT cellulose (like plants).</td></tr><tr><td>Fungi are NOT plants: they are heterotrophic, lack chlorophyll, and are in a separate kingdom.</td></tr><tr><td>Deuteromycetes are "imperfect" fungi — they have lost the sexual stage; they are not a true phylogenetic group.</td></tr><tr><td>Yeast (Saccharomyces) is an Ascomycete; it reproduces by budding (asexual) but also by forming ascospores.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhizopus (bread mould) is a Phycomycete; it reproduces by sporangiospores, not by sexual spores in the lab.</td></tr><tr><td>Mushrooms (Agaricus) are Basidiomycetes; the visible mushroom is the fruiting body of the mycelium.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Fungi are a distinct kingdom of eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms with chitin cell walls and absorptive nutrition. They are classified into four major groups — Phycomycetes (lower, aseptate), Ascomycetes (sac fungi), Basidiomycetes (club fungi), and Deuteromycetes (imperfect). Their roles as decomposers, food sources, medicine producers (antibiotics), and industrial agents make them ecologically and economically vital. NEB examinations focus on the four-group classification, structure of Penicillium, and economic importance.</p><hr><h2>Status of flora in Nepal and world representation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Nepal occupies under 0.1% of the world's land area but hosts about 2% of its flowering plants — over 7,000 vascular plant species have been recorded from the country.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Altitude drives Nepal's floral belts: tropical sal and chir pine forest in the Terai, oak–rhododendron and temperate conifer belts in the hills, birch–juniper scrub and alpine meadows above the tree line.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Endemism is high for a country of its size: roughly 3% of Nepal's flowering plants are endemic, concentrated in the central Himalaya (for example Himalayan hemlock and spiny berryl).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>World pattern: tropical rainforests cover under 10% of the earth's land but hold about two-thirds of all plant species; species richness falls steadily with increasing latitude and altitude.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Threats to flora include habitat loss, over-harvesting, invasive species and climate change; conservation uses in-situ methods (protected areas) together with ex-situ methods (seed banks, botanical gardens).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Status of flora in Nepal</td></tr><tr><td>• World representation</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Status of flora in Nepal" as one blob → ✅ split into: status of flora in nepal, world representation.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Status of flora in Nepal and world representation (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Status of flora in Nepal, world representation. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Algae</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Algae: photosynthetic, eukaryotic (mostly), aquatic or damp-environment organisms; no true roots, stems, or leaves.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Major groups: Chrysophyceae (diatoms, golden algae — silica cell walls), Pyrrophyceae (dinoflagellates — bioluminescence, red tides), Chlorophyceae (green algae — Chlorophyll a + b, starch storage).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (spores, fragmentation) and sexual (isogamous, anisogamous, oogamous).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ecological importance: primary producers in aquatic ecosystems; produce ~50% of Earth's oxygen; base of marine food chains.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Economic importance: food (agar, carrageenan, spirulina), industry (alginates, pigments), agriculture (biofertilisers — cyanobacteria in rice paddies).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>In NEB syllabus: algae are studied as non-vascular, photosynthetic organisms before bryophytes.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Algae are photosynthetic, mostly aquatic, lack true vascular tissues.</td></tr><tr><td>Three major groups: Chrysophyceae, Pyrrophyceae, Chlorophyceae.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological role: ~50% of Earth's O₂; base of aquatic food chains.</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction: asexual (spores, fragmentation) + sexual (isogamy, anisogamy, oogamy).</td></tr><tr><td>Economic uses: food, industry (agar, alginates), biofertilisers.</td></tr><tr><td>Cyanobacteria ≠ true algae (prokaryotic vs eukaryotic).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Algae are NOT plants in the strict sense: they lack true tissues, roots, and seeds. They are placed in Protista (or Plantae in some systems) depending on the classification used.</td></tr><tr><td>Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae) are prokaryotes, not true algae; they are in the domain Bacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>Dinoflagellates (Pyrrophyceae) can cause harmful algal blooms (red tides); most algae are harmless.</td></tr><tr><td>Chlorophyll content determines the group: green algae have chlorophyll a + b; brown algae (Phaeophyceae) have a + c + fucoxanthin.</td></tr><tr><td>Algal "stems" and "leaves" are not true vascular tissues; they are structural modifications.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Algae are photosynthetic, mostly aquatic organisms that lack true roots, stems, and leaves. They are classified into Chrysophyceae (diatoms, golden algae), Pyrrophyceae (dinoflagellates), and Chlorophyceae (green algae). As primary producers, algae generate about half of the world's oxygen and form the base of aquatic food webs. Their economic importance spans food, industry, and agriculture. NEB examinations focus on algal classification, ecological role, and economic uses.</p><hr><h2>Fungi: General introduction and characteristic features o…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**General features.** Fungi are achlorophyllous, heterotrophic, eukaryotic organisms whose body is a thallus made of thread-like hyphae (a mycelium); cell walls contain chitin and food is stored as glycogen.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Phycomycetes.** These lower fungi have aseptate (coenocytic) hyphae and form thick-walled zygospores sexually; examples are Mucor, Rhizopus and the plant pathogen Albugo.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Ascomycetes.** Sac fungi have septate hyphae and produce ascospores inside sac-like asci; Saccharomyces, Aspergillus, Penicillium and morels belong to this group.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Basidiomycetes.** Club fungi bear basidiospores on club-shaped basidia; Agaricus (button mushroom), Puccinia (wheat rust) and Ustilago (smut) are common examples.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Deuteromycetes.** The imperfect fungi show no known sexual stage and are classified by their conidia; Alternaria, Trichoderma and Colletotrichum are important members.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Fungi</td></tr><tr><td>• Characteristic features of phycomycetes</td></tr><tr><td>• Ascomycetes</td></tr><tr><td>• Basidiomycetes</td></tr><tr><td>• Deuteromycetes</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Fungi" as one blob → ✅ split into: fungi, characteristic features of phycomycetes, ascomycetes.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Fungi: General introduction and characteristic features o… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Fungi, characteristic features of phycomycetes, ascomycetes. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Bryophyta — Liverworts, Hornworts and Mosses</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>DETAILED BRYOPHYTE (Marchantia) STRUCTURE AND LIFE CYCLE.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Thallus: flat, ribbon-like, dichotomously branched; upper surface with air cavities (photorespiration) + photosynthetic chambers; lower surface with rhizoids (anchorage + absorption) + scales.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Gemma cups: dome-shaped, on the upper surface; contain gemmae (asexual, multicellular) that splash out and grow into new thalli.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sexual organs: antheridia (male, flask-shaped, at the base of the antheridiophore) produce biflagellated sperm; archegonia (female, neck + venter, at the base of the archegoniophore) produce a single egg.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Fertilisation: sperm swim through water to the egg in the archegonium → zygote (2n) in the venter.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sporophyte: dependent on the thallus; foot (attached) + seta (stalk) + capsule (sporangium) with elaters; meiosis in the spore mother cell → spores (n).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Spores germinate → new protonema → thallus (gametophyte) — the DOMINANT generation.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Life cycle: alternation of generations with gametophyte (n) dominant; sporophyte (2n) short-lived and nutritionally dependent.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Economic/ecological: pioneer on bare rock, soil formation, moisture retention, SO2 bio-indicator, ornamental; peat (from Sphagnum moss) used as fuel + soil conditioner.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Other bryophytes: mosses (Funaria, Polytrichum — cauloid with stem + leaf), liverworts (Marchantia — thalloid), hornworts (Anthoceros — horn-like elongating sporophyte).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>NEB focus: detailed Marchantia structure, asexual (gemmae) + sexual (antheridia/archegonia) reproduction, and the life cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Bryophytes: non-vascular land plants (no true xylem or phloem); include mosses, liverworts, and hornworts.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Life cycle: alternation of generations with the gametophyte (n) as the dominant, visible generation; the sporophyte (2n) is dependent on the gametophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Mosses (Bryophyta): e.g., Polytrichum, Funaria; stem-like (cauloid), leaf-like (phylloid) structures; no true roots (rhizoids instead).</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Liverworts (Marchantiophyta): thalloid (flat, ribbon-like) or leafy; Marchantia polymorpha is the model; gemmae cups for asexual reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Hornworts (Anthocerotophyta): thalloid; the sporophyte is horn-like and elongates from the base.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (fragmentation, gemmae) and sexual (flagellated sperm swim to egg in the archegonium; fertilisation requires water).</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Economic/ecological importance: pioneer species on bare rock, soil formation, moisture retention, indicators of air pollution (sensitive to SO₂), ornamental use.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>NEB focus: Marchantia structure (thalus, rhizoids, gemma cups, antheridia, archegonia) and life cycle.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Marchantia thallus: upper air cavities + lower rhizoids + scales.</td></tr><tr><td>Gemma cups: asexual reproduction via gemmae.</td></tr><tr><td>Antheridia (male) + archegonia (female) at the base of the stalked reproductive bodies.</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation requires water (flagellated sperm).</td></tr><tr><td>Sporophyte: foot + seta + capsule with elaters; dependent on the thallus.</td></tr><tr><td>Gametophyte (n) is the dominant generation.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological: pioneer, soil formation, moisture, SO2 bio-indicator; peat (Sphagnum).</td></tr><tr><td>Bryophytes: non-vascular (no xylem/phloem), rhizoids instead of roots.</td></tr><tr><td>Gametophyte (n) is the dominant, visible generation.</td></tr><tr><td>Sporophyte (2n) is dependent on the gametophyte for nutrition.</td></tr><tr><td>Three groups: mosses (Bryophyta), liverworts (Marchantiophyta), hornworts (Anthocerotophyta).</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction requires water (flagellated sperm); gemmae for asexual reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological role: pioneers, soil formation, moisture, air pollution indicators.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The thallus is NOT a true leaf or stem; it is a flattened gametophyte body.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhizoids are single-celled (in liverworts) or multicellular (in mosses) filaments; they are NOT true roots (no xylem/phloem).</td></tr><tr><td>Gemmae are asexual propagules; spores are meiotic products — they are different.</td></tr><tr><td>The sporophyte is dependent on the gametophyte (absorbs nutrients through the foot); it does NOT photosynthesise independently in Marchantia.</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation requires WATER for the flagellated sperm to swim — a key limitation of bryophytes.</td></tr><tr><td>The gametophyte (n) is the dominant, visible generation; the sporophyte (2n) is small and short-lived.</td></tr><tr><td>Bryophytes lack TRUE roots, stems, and leaves; they have rhizoids, cauloids, and phylloids instead.</td></tr><tr><td>The gametophyte is the DOMINANT generation in bryophytes (unlike seed plants where the sporophyte dominates).</td></tr><tr><td>The sporophyte is dependent on the gametophyte for nutrition (it does not photosynthesise independently in most mosses).</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation in bryophytes requires WATER for sperm to swim to the egg; this limits bryophytes to moist habitats.</td></tr><tr><td>Gemmae are asexual propagules in gemma cups; they are NOT spores.</td></tr><tr><td>Liverworts are NOT mosses: liverworts are thalloid (Marchantia) or leafy; mosses are cauloid (stem + leaf).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A detailed study of Marchantia (liverwort) reveals a flat, dichotomously branched thallus with upper air cavities and lower rhizoids. Asexual reproduction occurs through gemma cups that release gemmae. Sexual reproduction involves antheridia (sperm) and archegonia (egg) at the base of the stalked reproductive bodies; fertilisation requires water. The dependent sporophyte (foot + seta + capsule with elaters) produces spores by meiosis, which germinate into the dominant gametophyte generation. Bryophytes are ecologically important as pioneers, soil builders, moisture retainers, and SO2 bio-indicators; Sphagnum moss forms peat. NEB focuses on the detailed Marchantia structure, both reproduction modes, and the life cycle. Bryophytes are the simplest land plants: non-vascular, with the gametophyte as the dominant generation. They include mosses, liverworts, and hornworts. Marchantia (liverwort) and Funaria (moss) are the model organisms studied in NEB. Reproduction requires water for fertilisation; asexual reproduction occurs via gemmae and fragmentation. Bryophytes are ecologically important as pioneer species, soil builders, moisture retainers, and air-pollution indicators.</p><hr><h2>Bryophytes</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Bryophytes: non-vascular land plants (no true xylem or phloem); include mosses, liverworts, and hornworts.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Life cycle: alternation of generations with the gametophyte (n) as the dominant, visible generation; the sporophyte (2n) is dependent on the gametophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Mosses (Bryophyta): e.g., Polytrichum, Funaria; stem-like (cauloid), leaf-like (phylloid) structures; no true roots (rhizoids instead).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Liverworts (Marchantiophyta): thalloid (flat, ribbon-like) or leafy; Marchantia polymorpha is the model; gemmae cups for asexual reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Hornworts (Anthocerotophyta): thalloid; the sporophyte is horn-like and elongates from the base.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (fragmentation, gemmae) and sexual (flagellated sperm swim to egg in the archegonium; fertilisation requires water).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Economic/ecological importance: pioneer species on bare rock, soil formation, moisture retention, indicators of air pollution (sensitive to SO₂), ornamental use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>NEB focus: Marchantia structure (thalus, rhizoids, gemma cups, antheridia, archegonia) and life cycle.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Bryophytes: non-vascular (no xylem/phloem), rhizoids instead of roots.</td></tr><tr><td>Gametophyte (n) is the dominant, visible generation.</td></tr><tr><td>Sporophyte (2n) is dependent on the gametophyte for nutrition.</td></tr><tr><td>Three groups: mosses (Bryophyta), liverworts (Marchantiophyta), hornworts (Anthocerotophyta).</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction requires water (flagellated sperm); gemmae for asexual reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological role: pioneers, soil formation, moisture, air pollution indicators.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Bryophytes lack TRUE roots, stems, and leaves; they have rhizoids, cauloids, and phylloids instead.</td></tr><tr><td>The gametophyte is the DOMINANT generation in bryophytes (unlike seed plants where the sporophyte dominates).</td></tr><tr><td>The sporophyte is dependent on the gametophyte for nutrition (it does not photosynthesise independently in most mosses).</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation in bryophytes requires WATER for sperm to swim to the egg; this limits bryophytes to moist habitats.</td></tr><tr><td>Gemmae are asexual propagules in gemma cups; they are NOT spores.</td></tr><tr><td>Liverworts are NOT mosses: liverworts are thalloid (Marchantia) or leafy; mosses are cauloid (stem + leaf).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Bryophytes are the simplest land plants: non-vascular, with the gametophyte as the dominant generation. They include mosses, liverworts, and hornworts. Marchantia (liverwort) and Funaria (moss) are the model organisms studied in NEB. Reproduction requires water for fertilisation; asexual reproduction occurs via gemmae and fragmentation. Bryophytes are ecologically important as pioneer species, soil builders, moisture retainers, and air-pollution indicators.</p><hr><h2>Structure and reproduction of Mucor and Yeast</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Mucor thallus.** Mucor is a saprophytic mould with branched, aseptate (coenocytic) hyphae that grow on decaying organic matter such as bread and fruit.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Mucor reproduction.** Asexually it forms sporangiospores inside a globose sporangium; sexually, compatible hyphae fuse to produce a thick-walled zygospore.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Yeast structure.** Saccharomyces cerevisiae is a unicellular, oval eukaryote with a chitinous wall, a large vacuole and a single nucleus — it has no mycelium.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Yeast reproduction.** Yeast multiplies by budding and fission; under sexual conditions it forms ascospores inside an ascus, so it is classified with the ascomycetes.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Economic importance.** Yeast ferments sugars to ethyl alcohol and carbon dioxide (baking, brewing) and is a source of vitamin B and single-cell protein.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Structure</td></tr><tr><td>• Reproduction of Mucor</td></tr><tr><td>• Yeast</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Structure" as one blob → ✅ split into: structure, reproduction of mucor, yeast.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Structure and reproduction of Mucor and Yeast (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Structure, reproduction of Mucor, Yeast. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous and non-poisonous mu…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Basidiocarp.** The mushroom is the fleshy fruiting body of a basidiomycete, with a cap (pileus), stalk (stipe) and gills (lamellae) on which basidiospores are produced.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Edible mushrooms.** Agaricus bisporus (button mushroom) and Pleurotus (oyster mushroom) are cultivated widely and are rich in protein, vitamins and minerals.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Poisonous mushrooms.** Amanita muscaria (fly agaric) and other toxic species contain deadly toxins; misidentification can cause severe poisoning, so wild mushrooms must never be eaten unverified.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Ecological role.** Many mushrooms are decomposers, and forest trees depend on mycorrhizal fungal associations for mineral and water absorption.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Cultivation.** Mushroom farming uses composted substrate spawned with fungal mycelium under humid, shaded conditions — a growing cottage industry in Nepal.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Introduction of Mushrooms</td></tr><tr><td>• Poisonous</td></tr><tr><td>• Non-poisonous mushrooms</td></tr><tr><td>• Economic importance of fungi</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Introduction of Mushrooms" as one blob → ✅ split into: introduction of mushrooms, poisonous, non-poisonous mushrooms.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous and non-poisonous mu… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous, non-poisonous mushrooms. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Pteridophyta — Ferns and Allies</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>DETAILED PTERIDOPHYTE (Dryopteris) MORPHOLOGY AND REPRODUCTION.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Rhizome: short, creeping, bearing scales + adventitious roots; the main vegetative organ.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Frond: the leaf-like organ; stipe (stalk) + blade (lamina); blade divided into pinnae, each into pinules.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sori: clusters of sporangia on the underside of the pinnae; often covered by an indusium (a flap of tissue).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sporangium: bears an annulus (a row of specialized cells for dehiscence); meiosis in the spore mother cell → spores (n).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Spore: one-celled, motile-free, wind-dispersed; germinates in moist conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Prothallus (gametophyte): small, heart-shaped, free-living; bears antheridia (male) + archegonia (female); produces biflagellated sperm + egg.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Fertilisation: sperm swims through water to the egg → zygote (2n) → young sporophyte (dependent on the prothallus initially).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Young sporophyte: develops a rhizome + fronds; becomes independent; the prothallus withers.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Life cycle: alternation of generations with SPOROPHYTE (2n) dominant; gametophyte (n) small, free-living, and short-lived.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Key difference from bryophytes: true vascular tissue (xylem + phloem) + dominant sporophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Economic importance: medicinal (ferns in traditional medicine), ornamental (garden ferns), soil stabilisation; fossil pteridophytes form coal deposits.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Other pteridophytes: horsetails (Equisetum — jointed, silica), clubmosses (Lycopodium — cones), tree ferns (Cyathea).</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>NEB focus: detailed Dryopteris frond + rhizome structure, sori/annulus, the prothallus, and the full life cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Pteridophytes: seedless vascular plants (true xylem and phloem); include ferns, horsetails, clubmosses.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Life cycle: alternation of generations; the sporophyte (2n) is the dominant, visible generation; the gametophyte (n) is small, free-living, and short-lived.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Ferns: frond (leaf-like), rhizome (horizontal stem), stipe (leaf stalk), pinnae; spores in sori on the underside of fronds.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Dryopteris (model fern): rhizome, fronds, sori with sporangia; spores (n) germinate → prothallus (heart-shaped gametophyte) → antheridia and archegonia → fertilisation (requires water) → young sporophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (spores) and sexual (flagellated sperm + egg; requires water).</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Economic/ecological importance: medicinal (Asteraceae ferns used in traditional medicine), ornamental (garden ferns), soil stabilisation, indicators of humidity; fossils of pteridophytes form coal deposits.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Key difference from bryophytes: pteridophytes have TRUE vascular tissue (xylem + phloem) and a dominant sporophyte generation.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Dryopteris frond: stipe + blade; pinnae + pinules; sori on the underside.</td></tr><tr><td>Sorus: cluster of sporangia; annulus (dehiscent) + indusium (cover).</td></tr><tr><td>Prothallus: small, heart-shaped, free-living; antheridia + archegonia.</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation requires water (flagellated sperm).</td></tr><tr><td>Sporophyte (2n) is the dominant generation.</td></tr><tr><td>True vascular tissue (xylem + phloem) — the key difference from bryophytes.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic: medicinal, ornamental, soil; fossil coal.</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes: seedless, VASCULAR plants (true xylem + phloem).</td></tr><tr><td>Sporophyte (2n) is the dominant, visible generation.</td></tr><tr><td>Prothallus (n) is the small, free-living gametophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction: spores (asexual) + flagellated sperm (sexual, requires water).</td></tr><tr><td>Fern frond: stipe + blade; sori on underside with sporangia.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic: medicinal, ornamental, soil, fossil coal deposits.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The prothallus (gametophyte) is small and heart-shaped; it is NOT the main plant body (that is the frond-bearing sporophyte).</td></tr><tr><td>Sori are clusters of sporangia on the frond underside; the indusium covers them (not the sori themselves).</td></tr><tr><td>The annulus is the specialized dehiscent structure of the sporangium (a row of cells that snaps the capsule open).</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation STILL requires water in pteridophytes (flagellated sperm) — a key exam point.</td></tr><tr><td>The young sporophyte is initially dependent on the prothallus; it becomes independent as it develops its own rhizome + fronds.</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes have TRUE vascular tissue; bryophytes do NOT — the key distinction.</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes have TRUE vascular tissue (xylem and phloem); bryophytes do NOT. This is the key distinction.</td></tr><tr><td>The SPOROPHYTE is the dominant generation in pteridophytes (unlike bryophytes where the gametophyte dominates).</td></tr><tr><td>The prothallus (gametophyte) of a fern is small, heart-shaped, and free-living — NOT the main plant body.</td></tr><tr><td>Sori are clusters of sporangia on the underside of fern fronds; they are NOT the frond itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation still requires WATER in pteridophytes (flagellated sperm), even though the sporophyte is dominant.</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes do NOT produce seeds; they reproduce by spores. Gymnosperms and angiosperms produce seeds.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A detailed study of Dryopteris (fern) shows a short, creeping, scaled rhizome bearing adventitious roots and fronds. The frond (stipe + blade) has pinnae and pinules, with sori (clusters of sporangia with an annulus and indusium) on the underside. Meiosis in the spore mother cell produces spores that germinate into the small, heart-shaped prothallus (gametophyte), which bears antheridia and archegonia. Fertilisation requires water; the zygote develops into the dominant sporophyte. Pteridophytes are distinguished from bryophytes by their true vascular tissue and dominant sporophyte. NEB focuses on the detailed frond + rhizome structure, sori/annulus, prothallus, and the full life cycle. Pteridophytes are seedless vascular plants that represent the evolutionary transition between non-vascular bryophytes and seed-bearing plants. The sporophyte (2n) is the dominant generation; the gametophyte (prothallus) is small and free-living. Ferns like Dryopteris have fronds with sori (spore clusters). Fertilisation still requires water. Pteridophytes are economically important as medicinal herbs, ornamentals, soil stabilisers, and the source of coal deposits.</p><hr><h2>Pteridophytes</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Pteridophytes: seedless vascular plants (true xylem and phloem); include ferns, horsetails, clubmosses.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Life cycle: alternation of generations; the sporophyte (2n) is the dominant, visible generation; the gametophyte (n) is small, free-living, and short-lived.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ferns: frond (leaf-like), rhizome (horizontal stem), stipe (leaf stalk), pinnae; spores in sori on the underside of fronds.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Dryopteris (model fern): rhizome, fronds, sori with sporangia; spores (n) germinate → prothallus (heart-shaped gametophyte) → antheridia and archegonia → fertilisation (requires water) → young sporophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (spores) and sexual (flagellated sperm + egg; requires water).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Economic/ecological importance: medicinal (Asteraceae ferns used in traditional medicine), ornamental (garden ferns), soil stabilisation, indicators of humidity; fossils of pteridophytes form coal deposits.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Key difference from bryophytes: pteridophytes have TRUE vascular tissue (xylem + phloem) and a dominant sporophyte generation.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes: seedless, VASCULAR plants (true xylem + phloem).</td></tr><tr><td>Sporophyte (2n) is the dominant, visible generation.</td></tr><tr><td>Prothallus (n) is the small, free-living gametophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction: spores (asexual) + flagellated sperm (sexual, requires water).</td></tr><tr><td>Fern frond: stipe + blade; sori on underside with sporangia.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic: medicinal, ornamental, soil, fossil coal deposits.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes have TRUE vascular tissue (xylem and phloem); bryophytes do NOT. This is the key distinction.</td></tr><tr><td>The SPOROPHYTE is the dominant generation in pteridophytes (unlike bryophytes where the gametophyte dominates).</td></tr><tr><td>The prothallus (gametophyte) of a fern is small, heart-shaped, and free-living — NOT the main plant body.</td></tr><tr><td>Sori are clusters of sporangia on the underside of fern fronds; they are NOT the frond itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation still requires WATER in pteridophytes (flagellated sperm), even though the sporophyte is dominant.</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophytes do NOT produce seeds; they reproduce by spores. Gymnosperms and angiosperms produce seeds.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Pteridophytes are seedless vascular plants that represent the evolutionary transition between non-vascular bryophytes and seed-bearing plants. The sporophyte (2n) is the dominant generation; the gametophyte (prothallus) is small and free-living. Ferns like Dryopteris have fronds with sori (spore clusters). Fertilisation still requires water. Pteridophytes are economically important as medicinal herbs, ornamentals, soil stabilisers, and the source of coal deposits.</p><hr><h2>Gymnosperm â€” Pinus Structure and Reproduction</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>DETAILED GYMNOSPERM (Pinus) MORPHOLOGY AND REPRODUCTION.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Plant body: woody stem (trunk) + branch system; needle-like (Pinus) or scale-like (Deodar) leaves; no true flowers; reproduction via cones (strobili).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Male cone (microstrobilus): small, clustered at the base of new shoots; microsporangia → microspores (n) → pollen grains (male gametophyte) with an air-sac for wind dispersal.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Female cone (megasporangium / seed cone): larger, at the tip of shoots; megasporangia → megaspores (n) → female gametophyte (archegonia) on the ovule; the ovule is exposed on the cone scale (naked seed).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Pollination: wind (anemophilous); pollen lands on the resinous ovule; pollen tube grows to the archegonium; fertilisation (no water needed).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Seed: embryo + endosperm (n) + seed coat; no fruit (naked); dispersed by wind.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Life cycle: SPOROPHYTE (2n) dominant; gametophyte reduced, non-photosynthetic, and dependent on the sporophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Key differences from angiosperms: no flowers, no fruits, no double fertilisation; seeds exposed on cone scales.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Economic importance: timber (construction, paper), resin (turpentine from Pinus), ornamental (conifers in gardens), soil stabilisation in alpine regions; Deodar is a sacred tree in Nepali tradition.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Other gymnosperms: Ginkgo (living fossil, deciduous), Cycas (cycad, palm-like), Gnetum (angiosperm-like).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>NEB focus: detailed Pinus male + female cone structure, pollen, ovule, seed, and the life cycle; economic importance in Nepal.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Gymnosperms: seed plants with "naked" seeds (seeds not enclosed in a fruit); include conifers (Pinus, Deodar), ginkgo, cycads, and gnetophytes.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Body: woody stem (trunk), needle-like or scale-like leaves; no true flowers; reproduction via cones (strobili).</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Pinus (model): conifer; male cones (pollen cones) produce microspores → pollen grains; female cones (seed cones) produce ovules → seeds after pollination.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Life cycle: sporophyte (2n) dominant; microsporangia → microspores (n) → pollen (male gametophyte); megasporangia → megaspores (n) → female gametophyte; pollination → fertilisation (no water needed; wind-pollinated).</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Key differences from angiosperms: no flowers, no fruits, no double fertilisation; seeds are exposed on cone scales.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Economic importance: timber (Deodar, Pine for construction, paper), resin (turpentine from Pinus), ornamental (conifers in gardens), soil stabilisation in alpine regions.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>NEB focus: Pinus morphology (male and female cones) and reproduction; economic importance of gymnosperms in Nepal.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Pinus: woody, needle-like leaves, cones; no flowers or fruits.</td></tr><tr><td>Male cone: microsporangium → pollen (wind-dispersed).</td></tr><tr><td>Female cone: ovule on the cone scale → seed (naked).</td></tr><tr><td>Pollen: body + air-sac; two-celled; wind dispersal.</td></tr><tr><td>Life cycle: sporophyte (2n) dominant; gametophyte reduced.</td></tr><tr><td>No double fertilisation (unlike angiosperms).</td></tr><tr><td>Economic: timber, resin, ornamental, sacred (Deodar), soil stabilisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms: seeds NOT enclosed in fruit; naked seeds on cone scales.</td></tr><tr><td>No true flowers; reproduction via cones (strobili).</td></tr><tr><td>Pinus: wind-pollinated; male cones produce pollen; female cones bear ovules.</td></tr><tr><td>Life cycle: sporophyte dominant; gametophyte reduced, non-photosynthetic.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic: timber, resin, ornamental, soil, sacred trees (Deodar).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms produce SEEDS but NOT fruits; angiosperms produce both.</td></tr><tr><td>The male cone produces pollen (male gametophyte); the female cone bears the ovule → seed.</td></tr><tr><td>Pinus is wind-pollinated (anemophilous); no flowers, no nectar, no insects involved.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms do NOT undergo double fertilisation (angiosperm feature).</td></tr><tr><td>The gametophyte in gymnosperms is reduced and non-photosynthetic (unlike bryophytes where the gametophyte is dominant).</td></tr><tr><td>Deodar (Cedrus deodara) and Pine (Pinus) are both in the family Pinaceae; they differ in leaf form and cone structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms produce SEEDS but NOT fruits; angiosperms produce both seeds AND fruits.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperm seeds are "naked" (exposed on cone scales); angiosperm seeds are enclosed in a fruit.</td></tr><tr><td>Pinus is wind-pollinated (anemophilous); no flowers or nectar are involved.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms do NOT undergo double fertilisation; that is unique to angiosperms.</td></tr><tr><td>Deodar (Cedrus deodara) and Pine (Pinus) are both conifers in the family Pinaceae; they differ in leaf form (needle-like vs scale-like) and cone structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms have a dominant sporophyte; the gametophyte is reduced and non-photosynthetic.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A detailed study of Pinus (gymnosperm) reveals a woody plant with needle-like leaves and cones. The male cone bears microsporangia that produce pollen grains (each with a body and an air-sac for wind dispersal). The female cone bears the ovule on the cone scale; pollination is anemophilous (wind), and fertilisation does not require water. The resulting seed is "naked" (exposed on the cone scale, no fruit). The life cycle has a dominant sporophyte and a reduced, non-photosynthetic gametophyte. Gymnosperms are economically important in Nepal as timber (Deodar, Pine), resin sources, ornamentals, and sacred trees. NEB focuses on the detailed cone structures, pollen, ovule, seed, and the life cycle. Gymnosperms are seed plants with naked (exposed) seeds, not enclosed in fruits. They reproduce via cones; Pinus is the NEB model. Pollination is wind-based (anemophilous); no flowers or nectar are involved. The life cycle has a dominant sporophyte and a reduced gametophyte. Gymnosperms are economically important in Nepal as timber (Deodar, Pine), resin sources, ornamentals, and sacred trees. They differ from angiosperms in lacking flowers, fruits, and double fertilisation.</p><hr><h2>Gymnosperms</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Gymnosperms: seed plants with "naked" seeds (seeds not enclosed in a fruit); include conifers (Pinus, Deodar), ginkgo, cycads, and gnetophytes.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Body: woody stem (trunk), needle-like or scale-like leaves; no true flowers; reproduction via cones (strobili).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Pinus (model): conifer; male cones (pollen cones) produce microspores → pollen grains; female cones (seed cones) produce ovules → seeds after pollination.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Life cycle: sporophyte (2n) dominant; microsporangia → microspores (n) → pollen (male gametophyte); megasporangia → megaspores (n) → female gametophyte; pollination → fertilisation (no water needed; wind-pollinated).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Key differences from angiosperms: no flowers, no fruits, no double fertilisation; seeds are exposed on cone scales.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Economic importance: timber (Deodar, Pine for construction, paper), resin (turpentine from Pinus), ornamental (conifers in gardens), soil stabilisation in alpine regions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>NEB focus: Pinus morphology (male and female cones) and reproduction; economic importance of gymnosperms in Nepal.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms: seeds NOT enclosed in fruit; naked seeds on cone scales.</td></tr><tr><td>No true flowers; reproduction via cones (strobili).</td></tr><tr><td>Pinus: wind-pollinated; male cones produce pollen; female cones bear ovules.</td></tr><tr><td>Life cycle: sporophyte dominant; gametophyte reduced, non-photosynthetic.</td></tr><tr><td>No double fertilisation (unlike angiosperms).</td></tr><tr><td>Economic: timber, resin, ornamental, soil, sacred trees (Deodar).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms produce SEEDS but NOT fruits; angiosperms produce both seeds AND fruits.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperm seeds are "naked" (exposed on cone scales); angiosperm seeds are enclosed in a fruit.</td></tr><tr><td>Pinus is wind-pollinated (anemophilous); no flowers or nectar are involved.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms do NOT undergo double fertilisation; that is unique to angiosperms.</td></tr><tr><td>Deodar (Cedrus deodara) and Pine (Pinus) are both conifers in the family Pinaceae; they differ in leaf form (needle-like vs scale-like) and cone structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperms have a dominant sporophyte; the gametophyte is reduced and non-photosynthetic.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gymnosperms are seed plants with naked (exposed) seeds, not enclosed in fruits. They reproduce via cones; Pinus is the NEB model. Pollination is wind-based (anemophilous); no flowers or nectar are involved. The life cycle has a dominant sporophyte and a reduced gametophyte. Gymnosperms are economically important in Nepal as timber (Deodar, Pine), resin sources, ornamentals, and sacred trees. They differ from angiosperms in lacking flowers, fruits, and double fertilisation.</p><hr><h2>Lichen: General introduction, characteristic features and…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Symbiotic association.** A lichen is a partnership between a fungus (mycobiont) and a photosynthetic alga or cyanobacterium (phycobiont); the fungus supplies shelter and minerals while the partner supplies food.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Thallus types.** Lichens are crustose (crust-like, tightly attached), foliose (leafy, loosely attached) and fruticose (shrubby or pendant).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Pioneer vegetation.** Lichens colonise bare rock and gradually build soil, so they are pioneer species in primary succession.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Pollution indicator.** Lichens are extremely sensitive to sulphur dioxide and other air pollutants; their abundance signals clean air and their disappearance signals polluted air.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Economic importance.** Lichens yield litmus and dyes, provide fodder (reindeer moss), and produce antibiotic compounds such as usnic acid.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Lichen</td></tr><tr><td>• Characteristic features</td></tr><tr><td>• Economic importance of lichen</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Lichen" as one blob → ✅ split into: lichen, characteristic features, economic importance of lichen.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Lichen: General introduction, characteristic features and… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Lichen, characteristic features, economic importance of lichen. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Algae: General introduction and characteristic features o…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**General features.** Algae are simple, autotrophic, thalloid plants containing chlorophyll and other pigments; they occur in fresh water, marine water and moist soil and reproduce by spores and gametes.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Chlorophyceae (green algae).** They contain chlorophyll a and b, store food as starch in pyrenoids and include Chlamydomonas, Spirogyra and Chlorella.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Phaeophyceae (brown algae).** Fucoxanthin gives the brown colour; food is stored as mannitol and laminarin, and Fucus and Laminaria are marine examples.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Rhodophyceae (red algae).** Phycoerythrin masks the chlorophyll, food is stored as floridean starch, and Polysiphonia and Porphyra are common members.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Economic importance.** Algae produce much of the world's oxygen, yield agar, carrageenan and iodine, serve directly as food (Porphyra), and provide single-cell protein (Chlorella, Spirulina).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Algae</td></tr><tr><td>• Characteristic features of green</td></tr><tr><td>• Brown</td></tr><tr><td>• Red algae</td></tr><tr><td>• Structure</td></tr><tr><td>• Reproduction of Spirogyra</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Algae" as one blob → ✅ split into: algae, characteristic features of green, brown.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Algae: General introduction and characteristic features o… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Algae, characteristic features of green, brown. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Angiosperm â€” Morphology and Taxonomic Study</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Angiosperms (flowering plants): seeds enclosed in a fruit; include ~90% of all plant species; the most diverse and successful plant group.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Flower structure: perianth (sepals + petals), androecium (stamens: anther + filament), gynoecium/pistil (carpel: ovary + style + stigma).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Root: taproot (dicots) or fibrous (monocots); functions: absorption, anchorage, storage.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Stem: herbaceous (soft) or woody (hard); functions: support, transport (xylem up, phloem down).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Leaf: blade + petiole; venation (pinnate, palmate, parallel); stomata for gas exchange.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Inflorescence: arrangement of flowers on a peduncle (raceme, corymb, umbel, spike, capitulum).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fruit: mature ovary; types: simple (pea, apple), aggregate (raspberry), multiple (pineapple).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Taxonomic hierarchy: Division > Class > Order > Family > Genus > Species.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>NEB families: Brassicaceae (mustard, cabbage — 4 petals, cross shape), Fabaceae (pea, bean — zygomorphic, pod), Solanaceae (tomato, potato — 5 petals, bell shape), Liliaceae (tulip, onion — 6 tepals, monocot).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Classification systems: Artificial (Linnaeus — based on few characters), Natural (De Candolle, Bentham & Hooker — based on many characters), Phylogenetic (Engler, Takhtajan — based on evolutionary relationships).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Angiosperms: seeds enclosed in fruit; ~300,000 species.</td></tr><tr><td>Flower: sepal + petal + stamen (male) + carpel (female).</td></tr><tr><td>Monocots vs dicots: root, venation, flower parts, cotyledon number.</td></tr><tr><td>NEB families: Brassicaceae (4 petals), Fabaceae (zygomorphic), Solanaceae (5 petals), Liliaceae (6 tepals).</td></tr><tr><td>Taxonomic hierarchy: Division > Class > Order > Family > Genus > Species.</td></tr><tr><td>Classification systems: Artificial, Natural, Phylogenetic.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Angiosperms produce BOTH seeds AND fruits; gymnosperms produce seeds only (no fruit).</td></tr><tr><td>Monocots have parallel venation, fibrous roots, and 3-parted flowers; dicots have net venation, taproots, and 4-or-5-parted flowers.</td></tr><tr><td>The carpel (gynoecium) is the female part of the flower; the stamen is the male part.</td></tr><tr><td>Brassicaceae flowers have 4 petals in a cross shape (cruciform); Solanaceae have 5 petals (rotate-symmetric).</td></tr><tr><td>Fabaceae flowers are zygomorphic (bilaterally symmetric); most other angiosperm flowers are actinomorphic (radially symmetric).</td></tr><tr><td>An inflorescence is the arrangement of flowers; it is NOT a single flower.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angiosperms are the most diverse and successful plant group, with seeds enclosed in fruits. The flower is the reproductive organ, consisting of sepals, petals, stamens (male), and carpels (female). Monocots and dicots differ in root type, leaf venation, flower structure, and cotyledon number. NEB focuses on four families — Brassicaceae, Fabaceae, Solanaceae, and Liliaceae — and their economic importance. The taxonomic hierarchy and classification systems (artificial, natural, phylogenetic) complete the angiosperm unit.</p><hr><h2>Angiosperms</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Angiosperms (flowering plants): seeds enclosed in a fruit; include ~90% of all plant species; the most diverse and successful plant group.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Flower structure: perianth (sepals + petals), androecium (stamens: anther + filament), gynoecium/pistil (carpel: ovary + style + stigma).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Root: taproot (dicots) or fibrous (monocots); functions: absorption, anchorage, storage.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Stem: herbaceous (soft) or woody (hard); functions: support, transport (xylem up, phloem down).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Leaf: blade + petiole; venation (pinnate, palmate, parallel); stomata for gas exchange.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Inflorescence: arrangement of flowers on a peduncle (raceme, corymb, umbel, spike, capitulum).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fruit: mature ovary; types: simple (pea, apple), aggregate (raspberry), multiple (pineapple).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Taxonomic hierarchy: Division > Class > Order > Family > Genus > Species.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>NEB families: Brassicaceae (mustard, cabbage — 4 petals, cross shape), Fabaceae (pea, bean — zygomorphic, pod), Solanaceae (tomato, potato — 5 petals, bell shape), Liliaceae (tulip, onion — 6 tepals, monocot).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Classification systems: Artificial (Linnaeus — based on few characters), Natural (De Candolle, Bentham & Hooker — based on many characters), Phylogenetic (Engler, Takhtajan — based on evolutionary relationships).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Angiosperms: seeds enclosed in fruit; ~300,000 species.</td></tr><tr><td>Flower: sepal + petal + stamen (male) + carpel (female).</td></tr><tr><td>Monocots vs dicots: root, venation, flower parts, cotyledon number.</td></tr><tr><td>NEB families: Brassicaceae (4 petals), Fabaceae (zygomorphic), Solanaceae (5 petals), Liliaceae (6 tepals).</td></tr><tr><td>Taxonomic hierarchy: Division > Class > Order > Family > Genus > Species.</td></tr><tr><td>Classification systems: Artificial, Natural, Phylogenetic.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Angiosperms produce BOTH seeds AND fruits; gymnosperms produce seeds only (no fruit).</td></tr><tr><td>Monocots have parallel venation, fibrous roots, and 3-parted flowers; dicots have net venation, taproots, and 4-or-5-parted flowers.</td></tr><tr><td>The carpel (gynoecium) is the female part of the flower; the stamen is the male part.</td></tr><tr><td>Brassicaceae flowers have 4 petals in a cross shape (cruciform); Solanaceae have 5 petals (rotate-symmetric).</td></tr><tr><td>Fabaceae flowers are zygomorphic (bilaterally symmetric); most other angiosperm flowers are actinomorphic (radially symmetric).</td></tr><tr><td>An inflorescence is the arrangement of flowers; it is NOT a single flower.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angiosperms are the most diverse and successful plant group, with seeds enclosed in fruits. The flower is the reproductive organ, consisting of sepals, petals, stamens (male), and carpels (female). Monocots and dicots differ in root type, leaf venation, flower structure, and cotyledon number. NEB focuses on four families — Brassicaceae, Fabaceae, Solanaceae, and Liliaceae — and their economic importance. The taxonomic hierarchy and classification systems (artificial, natural, phylogenetic) complete the angiosperm unit.</p><hr><h2>Bryophyta: General introduction and characteristic featur…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Amphibians of the plant kingdom.** Bryophytes live in damp places and need water for fertilisation because the male gametes swim to the egg, yet they lack true vascular tissue.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Gametophyte dominance.** The leafy or thalloid gametophyte is the persistent phase; the sporophyte is small, short-lived and remains attached to it.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Sex organs.** Gametes are produced in multicellular antheridia (sperm) and archegonia (egg), both embedded in the gametophyte tissue.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Examples.** Marchantia is a thalloid liverwort, Anthoceros a hornwort, and Funaria a moss with a leafy gametophyte and a seta–capsule sporophyte.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Importance.** Sphagnum peat is used as fuel and packing material, bryophytes hold moisture and prevent soil erosion, and they indicate humid habitats.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Bryophyta</td></tr><tr><td>• Characteristic features of liverworts</td></tr><tr><td>• Hornworts</td></tr><tr><td>• Moss</td></tr><tr><td>• Morphological structure</td></tr><tr><td>• Reproduction of Marchantia</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Bryophyta" as one blob → ✅ split into: bryophyta, characteristic features of liverworts, hornworts.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Bryophyta: General introduction and characteristic featur… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Bryophyta, characteristic features of liverworts, hornworts. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Pteridophyta: General introduction and characteristic fea…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**First vascular plants.** Pteridophytes possess true roots, stems and leaves with xylem and phloem, which allowed plants to grow tall and colonise land fully.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Sporophyte dominance.** The conspicuous plant is the diploid sporophyte; the tiny gametophyte (prothallus) is short-lived and requires water for fertilisation.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Spore-bearing structures.** Spores are produced in sporangia clustered into sori on the undersurface of sporophylls.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Homosporous vs heterosporous.** Lycopodium, Equisetum and Pteris produce one kind of spore, while Selaginella, Marsilea and Salvinia produce two kinds (microspores and megaspores).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Importance.** Ferns are ornamentals (Adiantum, Nephrolepis), some pteridophytes serve as food and medicine, and their ancient forms contributed to coal deposits.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Pteridophyta</td></tr><tr><td>• Characteristic features of pteridophytes</td></tr><tr><td>• Morphological structure</td></tr><tr><td>• Reproduction of Dryopteris</td></tr><tr><td>• Economic importance of pteridophytes</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Pteridophyta" as one blob → ✅ split into: pteridophyta, characteristic features of pteridophytes, morphological structure.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Pteridophyta: General introduction and characteristic fea… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Pteridophyta, characteristic features of pteridophytes, morphological structure. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Gymnosperm: General introduction and characteristic featu…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Naked seeds.** Gymnosperms bear seeds that are not enclosed in a fruit; ovules lie exposed on the surface of megasporophylls, usually in cones.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Plant body.** They are mostly evergreen, woody trees and shrubs with well-developed roots (often with mycorrhizae) and needle-like or scale-like leaves.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Reproduction.** Male cones release pollen carried by wind to female cones; the ovule contains the female gametophyte and fertilisation gives a seed with a stored food supply.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Examples.** Cycas, Pinus, Cedrus (deodar), Ginkgo and Ephedra are familiar gymnosperms; Cycas retains fern-like leaflets and coralloid roots with nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Economic importance.** Gymnosperms give timber, resins, turpentine, ephedrine (medicine) and ornamentals; their pollen can also cause allergies.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Gymnosperm</td></tr><tr><td>• Characteristic features of Gymnosperms</td></tr><tr><td>• Morphology</td></tr><tr><td>• Reproduction of Pinus</td></tr><tr><td>• Economic importance of gymnosperm</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Gymnosperm" as one blob → ✅ split into: gymnosperm, characteristic features of gymnosperms, morphology.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gymnosperm: General introduction and characteristic featu… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Gymnosperm, characteristic features of Gymnosperms, morphology. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Angiosperm: Morphology of root, stem, leaves, inflorescen…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Root systems.** Roots are taproot (mustard), fibrous (grass) or adventitious (banyan prop roots); they anchor the plant, absorb water and minerals and often store food.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Stem.** The stem bears nodes, internodes, leaves and buds, and may be modified for storage (potato tuber), support (tendrils) or protection (thorns).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Leaves.** Leaves show simple or compound lamina, reticulate (dicot) or parallel (monocot) venation, and arrangements called phyllotaxy (alternate, opposite, whorled).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Inflorescence.** Flowers are arranged as racemose (acropetal succession) or cymose (basipetal succession) inflorescences on the floral axis.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Flower and fruit.** A complete flower has calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium; after fertilisation the ovary becomes a fruit whose pericarp encloses the seeds.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Angiosperm</td></tr><tr><td>• Morphology of root</td></tr><tr><td>• Stem</td></tr><tr><td>• Leaves</td></tr><tr><td>• Inflorescences</td></tr><tr><td>• Flowers</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Angiosperm" as one blob → ✅ split into: angiosperm, morphology of root, stem.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angiosperm: Morphology of root, stem, leaves, inflorescen… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Angiosperm, Morphology of root, stem. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Taxonomic study: Definition, taxonomic hierarchy, classif…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Taxonomy.** Taxonomy is the branch of biology that identifies, describes, classifies and names organisms; systematics additionally studies their evolutionary relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Hierarchy of categories.** Classification follows the sequence Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species, with species as the fundamental unit.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Binomial nomenclature.** Each species gets a two-word Latin name (genus + species) in italics or underlined, with the genus capitalised, as in Panthera tigris.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Systems of classification.** Artificial systems (Linnaeus) use a few visible characters, natural systems (Bentham and Hooker) use many characters, and phylogenetic systems use evolutionary relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Taxonomic aids.** Herbaria, botanical gardens, museums, keys and manuals help identify and study organisms; a key narrows identity through paired contrasting characters.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Taxonomic study</td></tr><tr><td>• Definition</td></tr><tr><td>• Taxonomic hierarchy</td></tr><tr><td>• Classification systems</td></tr><tr><td>• Artificial</td></tr><tr><td>• Natural</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Taxonomic study" as one blob → ✅ split into: taxonomic study, definition, taxonomic hierarchy.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Taxonomic study: Definition, taxonomic hierarchy, classif… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Taxonomic study, Definition, taxonomic hierarchy. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicacea…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Brassicaceae (Cruciferae).** Mustard family: flowers with four petals in a cross, six stamens (four long, two short), and silique fruits; mustard oil gives the pungent taste.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Fabaceae (Papilionoideae).** Pea family: papilionaceous corolla (standard, wings, keel), ten stamens (nine fused), legume fruits, and root nodules housing nitrogen-fixing Rhizobium.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Solanaceae.** Potato family: five epipetalous stamens with swollen anthers, bicarpellary superior ovary, and berry or capsule fruits; tomato, brinjal and chilli belong here.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Liliaceae.** Lily family: monocots with parallel-veined leaves, trimerous flowers with six tepals, and superior trilocular ovary; onion, garlic and tulip are examples.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Economic importance.** These four families supply food crops, spices, medicines (alkaloids), oils and ornamentals — they are among the most useful angiosperm families.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicaceae</td></tr><tr><td>• Fabaceae</td></tr><tr><td>• Solanaceae</td></tr><tr><td>• Liliaceae</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicaceae" as one blob → ✅ split into: angiosperm families with economic importance: brassicaceae, fabaceae, solanaceae.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicacea… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicaceae, Fabaceae, Solanaceae. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Introduction: Three domains of life, binomial nomenclatur…</td><td>Introduction: Three domains of life, binomial nomenclatur… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Introduction, Three domains of life, binomial nomenclature. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Three Domains and Classification</td><td>Modern taxonomy classifies life using two complementary systems: the three-domain system (Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya) based on molecular evidence, and the five-kingdom system (Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia) based on cell structure and nutrition. The taxonomic hierarchy (Domain → Species) and binomial nomenclature (Genus species) provide universal naming. Plant evolution shows a progressive transition from simple algae to complex angiosperms, with alternation of generations as a unifying feature.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Fungi â€” General Features and Types</td><td>Fungi are a distinct kingdom of eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms with chitin cell walls and absorptive nutrition. They are classified into four major groups — Phycomycetes (lower, aseptate), Ascomycetes (sac fungi), Basidiomycetes (club fungi), and Deuteromycetes (imperfect). Their roles as decomposers, food sources, medicine producers (antibiotics), and industrial agents make them ecologically and economically vital. NEB examinations focus on the four-group classification, structure of Penicillium, and economic importance.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Fungi</td><td>Fungi are a distinct kingdom of eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms with chitin cell walls and absorptive nutrition. They are classified into four major groups — Phycomycetes (lower, aseptate), Ascomycetes (sac fungi), Basidiomycetes (club fungi), and Deuteromycetes (imperfect). Their roles as decomposers, food sources, medicine producers (antibiotics), and industrial agents make them ecologically and economically vital. NEB examinations focus on the four-group classification, structure of Penicillium, and economic importance.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Status of flora in Nepal and world representation</td><td>Status of flora in Nepal and world representation (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Status of flora in Nepal, world representation. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Algae</td><td>Algae are photosynthetic, mostly aquatic organisms that lack true roots, stems, and leaves. They are classified into Chrysophyceae (diatoms, golden algae), Pyrrophyceae (dinoflagellates), and Chlorophyceae (green algae). As primary producers, algae generate about half of the world's oxygen and form the base of aquatic food webs. Their economic importance spans food, industry, and agriculture. NEB examinations focus on algal classification, ecological role, and economic uses.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Fungi: General introduction and characteristic features o…</td><td>Fungi: General introduction and characteristic features o… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Fungi, characteristic features of phycomycetes, ascomycetes. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Bryophyta — Liverworts, Hornworts and Mosses</td><td>A detailed study of Marchantia (liverwort) reveals a flat, dichotomously branched thallus with upper air cavities and lower rhizoids. Asexual reproduction occurs through gemma cups that release gemmae. Sexual reproduction involves antheridia (sperm) and archegonia (egg) at the base of the stalked reproductive bodies; fertilisation requires water. The dependent sporophyte (foot + seta + capsule with elaters) produces spores by meiosis, which germinate into the dominant gametophyte generation. Bryophytes are ecologically important as pioneers, soil builders, moisture retainers, and SO2 bio-indicators; Sphagnum moss forms peat. NEB focuses on the detailed Marchantia structure, both reproduction modes, and the life cycle. Bryophytes are the simplest land plants: non-vascular, with the gametophyte as the dominant generation. They include mosses, liverworts, and hornworts. Marchantia (liverwort) and Funaria (moss) are the model organisms studied in NEB. Reproduction requires water for fertilisation; asexual reproduction occurs via gemmae and fragmentation. Bryophytes are ecologically important as pioneer species, soil builders, moisture retainers, and air-pollution indicators.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Bryophytes</td><td>Bryophytes are the simplest land plants: non-vascular, with the gametophyte as the dominant generation. They include mosses, liverworts, and hornworts. Marchantia (liverwort) and Funaria (moss) are the model organisms studied in NEB. Reproduction requires water for fertilisation; asexual reproduction occurs via gemmae and fragmentation. Bryophytes are ecologically important as pioneer species, soil builders, moisture retainers, and air-pollution indicators.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Structure and reproduction of Mucor and Yeast</td><td>Structure and reproduction of Mucor and Yeast (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Structure, reproduction of Mucor, Yeast. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>11: Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous and non-poisonous mu…</td><td>Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous and non-poisonous mu… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Introduction of Mushrooms, poisonous, non-poisonous mushrooms. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>12: Pteridophyta — Ferns and Allies</td><td>A detailed study of Dryopteris (fern) shows a short, creeping, scaled rhizome bearing adventitious roots and fronds. The frond (stipe + blade) has pinnae and pinules, with sori (clusters of sporangia with an annulus and indusium) on the underside. Meiosis in the spore mother cell produces spores that germinate into the small, heart-shaped prothallus (gametophyte), which bears antheridia and archegonia. Fertilisation requires water; the zygote develops into the dominant sporophyte. Pteridophytes are distinguished from bryophytes by their true vascular tissue and dominant sporophyte. NEB focuses on the detailed frond + rhizome structure, sori/annulus, prothallus, and the full life cycle. Pteridophytes are seedless vascular plants that represent the evolutionary transition between non-vascular bryophytes and seed-bearing plants. The sporophyte (2n) is the dominant generation; the gametophyte (prothallus) is small and free-living. Ferns like Dryopteris have fronds with sori (spore clusters). Fertilisation still requires water. Pteridophytes are economically important as medicinal herbs, ornamentals, soil stabilisers, and the source of coal deposits.</td></tr><tr><td>13: Pteridophytes</td><td>Pteridophytes are seedless vascular plants that represent the evolutionary transition between non-vascular bryophytes and seed-bearing plants. The sporophyte (2n) is the dominant generation; the gametophyte (prothallus) is small and free-living. Ferns like Dryopteris have fronds with sori (spore clusters). Fertilisation still requires water. Pteridophytes are economically important as medicinal herbs, ornamentals, soil stabilisers, and the source of coal deposits.</td></tr><tr><td>14: Gymnosperm â€” Pinus Structure and Reproduction</td><td>A detailed study of Pinus (gymnosperm) reveals a woody plant with needle-like leaves and cones. The male cone bears microsporangia that produce pollen grains (each with a body and an air-sac for wind dispersal). The female cone bears the ovule on the cone scale; pollination is anemophilous (wind), and fertilisation does not require water. The resulting seed is "naked" (exposed on the cone scale, no fruit). The life cycle has a dominant sporophyte and a reduced, non-photosynthetic gametophyte. Gymnosperms are economically important in Nepal as timber (Deodar, Pine), resin sources, ornamentals, and sacred trees. NEB focuses on the detailed cone structures, pollen, ovule, seed, and the life cycle. Gymnosperms are seed plants with naked (exposed) seeds, not enclosed in fruits. They reproduce via cones; Pinus is the NEB model. Pollination is wind-based (anemophilous); no flowers or nectar are involved. The life cycle has a dominant sporophyte and a reduced gametophyte. Gymnosperms are economically important in Nepal as timber (Deodar, Pine), resin sources, ornamentals, and sacred trees. They differ from angiosperms in lacking flowers, fruits, and double fertilisation.</td></tr><tr><td>15: Gymnosperms</td><td>Gymnosperms are seed plants with naked (exposed) seeds, not enclosed in fruits. They reproduce via cones; Pinus is the NEB model. Pollination is wind-based (anemophilous); no flowers or nectar are involved. The life cycle has a dominant sporophyte and a reduced gametophyte. Gymnosperms are economically important in Nepal as timber (Deodar, Pine), resin sources, ornamentals, and sacred trees. They differ from angiosperms in lacking flowers, fruits, and double fertilisation.</td></tr><tr><td>16: Lichen: General introduction, characteristic features and…</td><td>Lichen: General introduction, characteristic features and… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Lichen, characteristic features, economic importance of lichen. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>17: Algae: General introduction and characteristic features o…</td><td>Algae: General introduction and characteristic features o… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Algae, characteristic features of green, brown. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>18: Angiosperm â€” Morphology and Taxonomic Study</td><td>Angiosperms are the most diverse and successful plant group, with seeds enclosed in fruits. The flower is the reproductive organ, consisting of sepals, petals, stamens (male), and carpels (female). Monocots and dicots differ in root type, leaf venation, flower structure, and cotyledon number. NEB focuses on four families — Brassicaceae, Fabaceae, Solanaceae, and Liliaceae — and their economic importance. The taxonomic hierarchy and classification systems (artificial, natural, phylogenetic) complete the angiosperm unit.</td></tr><tr><td>19: Angiosperms</td><td>Angiosperms are the most diverse and successful plant group, with seeds enclosed in fruits. The flower is the reproductive organ, consisting of sepals, petals, stamens (male), and carpels (female). Monocots and dicots differ in root type, leaf venation, flower structure, and cotyledon number. NEB focuses on four families — Brassicaceae, Fabaceae, Solanaceae, and Liliaceae — and their economic importance. The taxonomic hierarchy and classification systems (artificial, natural, phylogenetic) complete the angiosperm unit.</td></tr><tr><td>20: Bryophyta: General introduction and characteristic featur…</td><td>Bryophyta: General introduction and characteristic featur… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Bryophyta, characteristic features of liverworts, hornworts. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>21: Pteridophyta: General introduction and characteristic fea…</td><td>Pteridophyta: General introduction and characteristic fea… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Pteridophyta, characteristic features of pteridophytes, morphological structure. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>22: Gymnosperm: General introduction and characteristic featu…</td><td>Gymnosperm: General introduction and characteristic featu… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Gymnosperm, characteristic features of Gymnosperms, morphology. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>23: Angiosperm: Morphology of root, stem, leaves, inflorescen…</td><td>Angiosperm: Morphology of root, stem, leaves, inflorescen… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Angiosperm, Morphology of root, stem. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>24: Taxonomic study: Definition, taxonomic hierarchy, classif…</td><td>Taxonomic study: Definition, taxonomic hierarchy, classif… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Taxonomic study, Definition, taxonomic hierarchy. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>25: Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicacea…</td><td>Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicacea… (Floral Diversity, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Angiosperm families with economic importance: Brassicaceae, Fabaceae, Solanaceae. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Floral Diversity.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Ecosystems contain interacting organisms and non-living conditions. Energy flows through food webs and is dissipated as heat, while elements such as carbon and nitrogen cycle; biodiversity includes genetic, species and ecosystem diversity.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Population or ecosystem trends depend on place, season, sampling method and time scale. Distinguish a measured observation from a projection, and report uncertainty rather than treating one survey as a complete census.</p><h3>Open research question — How Himalayan ecosystems respond to rapid change</h3><p>Changes in snow and ice across the Hindu Kush Himalaya are documented, but timing and local consequences for water, habitats and particular species remain uncertain. Scenario-based projections are not guaranteed outcomes; monitoring and stating the scenario are essential.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Floral Diversity”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “How Himalayan ecosystems respond to rapid change”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems">OpenStax Biology: ecology of ecosystems</a></li><li><a href="https://hkh.icimod.org/hi-wise/">ICIMOD: HI-WISE assessment</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>